{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-160604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}-u,\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}-u,\\,uv) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-u,\\,u(1)) \\\\  & =(1-u,\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-t"] ["math" "1-t"] ["math" "1-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(2-u),\\,y=u{v}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $1\\ge u\\ge 0$ and $v=1.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v(2-u),\\,u{v}^{3}) \\\\  & =(1(2-u),\\,u(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =(2-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2+t"] ["math" "2-t"] ["math" "2t"] ["math" "t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(1+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=y(t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(1+v)) \\\\  & =(0-v,\\,0(1+v)) \\\\  & =(-v,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=0,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=0,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2t"] ["math" "t"] ["math" "t+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{0}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "1-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u(u+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,u(u+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,1(1+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,1+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=1+v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "1-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "2t"] ["math" "t"] ["math" "t-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=(u-1)v,\\,y=uv-1.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u-1)v,\\,uv-1) \\\\  & =((0-1)v,\\,(0)v-1) \\\\  & =(-v,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=-1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/48ae89e14005e5fc21f0693e769e6214c1c9a94ba0cd63e7ce08d9771fbf82f1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$"] ["text" "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-{v}^{2},\\,y=2(u-v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2(u-v)) \\\\  & =(u-{0}^{2},\\,2(u-0)) \\\\  & =(u,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/9d9c8e967d4290f0a75e071762ff05c97b12367f43f8a1a965fff09efa64743b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,2)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,2)$ and $(1,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,2)$ and $(0,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+4v,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+4v,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u)) \\\\  & =(u+4(0),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(0-4u)) \\\\  & =(u,-2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=-2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/9af81ffe277395ce00ef597235bb8f440ff22e5da9db5cbe3266dae8ba0fe281.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(-2,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,-2)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(1,0)$ and $(0,-2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+2u,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+2u,\\,v-u) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2u,\\,1-u) \\\\  & =(1+2u,\\,1-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1+2u,\\,y=1-u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1+2t,\\,y(t)=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/f75f8beceedef7d64d12c7cf1d4bc838039ecdeae3de752c45e76975f116fcb2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb