veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(0+6u),\\,\\frac{{0}^{3}-2u}{0+2}) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/f068883d1318acdafba39c74bc1279f6cafe803437a9b79249999df461bac8ee.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$"] ["text" "$(2,-1)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(2,0)$ and $(0,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,y=(u+v-1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,(u+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{0+1},\\,(0+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,(v-1)^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=(v-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (\\frac{1-1}{0+1},(0+1-1)^{2}+1)=(0,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (\\frac{0-1}{0+1},(0+0-1)^{2}+1)=(-1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/69a2a8ab136360c01d1a17a58dc2fc4864ae2089330a551df913b200d923f087.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from   $(1,1)$ to $(0,2).$"] ["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$"] ["text" "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,2)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=1-u+v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-u+v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-1+v) \\\\  & =(1-{v}^{2},\\,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-{v}^{2},\\,y=v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=1-x\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2},1-1+0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}-{1}^{0},1-1+1)=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/995b106a25173b4e3667fd3fce04f499d7d4a549ae76414328f6e3c406ddd3c6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(1-u-v),\\,y=\\frac{1-v}{1+u}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,⇒\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=(u-v)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,(u-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,(0-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1,(0-1)^{2})=(1,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (0+0,(0-0)^{2})=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/d5238b62454c6422988e55f0f4ab9183387725fed037df2a688d351b568ea5c1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(u-{v}^{3}).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(u-{v}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u-0,\\,u(u-{0}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={u}^{2},\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=y\\,⇒\\,y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0-0,\\,0(0-{0}^{3}))=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1-0,\\,1(1-{0}^{3}))=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7aa56f8bba41a352a02c6d26554c417e7844cd60c28078da51cbf16a3220e57d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$"] ["text" "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-225158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/2523e3cb51fea9c58b2076d69c89d21503341c6cbd4ca0db56d6b92455a40340.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/a687a8434e9643eabcdf3e1600a28eb805f35fd7821936effcb28677366e91ef.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/aa0d4fc0d276619b071a32fe727e7647ff29700bb34ec840a93cacc9088db1af.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/be160b4308b9bc676e1fb8033a1200e5af818007cc1f48661339b1c8d29a58db.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/12ded9e8002035e126364e41d0dffc1abd6332b3496b4db9da4a68e116e7492e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/25f5503b720084033be843eb1bce7adfe3bc59c39f5f634074d4e414079c9c36.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33293e18f3b42154b3a21dd627e7f06fbcf443b1251ac1a9ce6e5cb6a47c5971.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/4367227477398d54e05d978d1499aea7f0dabb42ab9223b21cbec43c5ada55c2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,{0}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v+1,\\,{v}^{2}-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,{1}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1-{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/225339084f34f53e3a406bb8804d31c0fb5f288c6d4456125926a0d0b1116122.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/cdd115f6defaabc72aaaeb8a6a1642e088860f281a847acacdb635f71a26be08.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e7c699d1d96e60f031a6fb006e4ae5ae1abd9898f574ab075d4e4a15a4958634.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/fc78afa81f267849dd5546919074c8bfcab6b35430358abaa6e2c9567c599ee0.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,{1}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,1-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-0,\\,{0}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/4ee5f1df8f74ca6412d4f02fba7258b2b672a8bc80ef13617d4b327f8859e007.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/662c1937641b84653920b9c4e8cfac8168470d077641d001ebd258a0ffff34ac.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6d514d79a788e4512370310eab371dc334e37fc20aaffdf3ad93ebe632b84d4b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/941f34cbed3f3d6a9957b8520b36d6cbdc20c6241d2e407381ac86d3241a3349.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v-u),\\,y=-u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(0-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(1(v-1),\\,-1) &  \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(1-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(0(v-0),\\,-0) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "232209e7428ebab9af73f974bd8a2f762375717a02282a0e417acaa1ccccb3b0"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1eeaf3fc507fa7cb5ef5e28e05abf76f113e8253872446bc69c1640022ec780e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/25098bb3cdab08abbfcc68e2a703e38df2abc07e562f6bfddee9e219135933e0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/589601eccdc398c685c3d595689be60a3482df49e592292a620e9380b0664419.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e9bbbef3a9ba9bbb3e305201281c82a46fe139972451686c8571cfc4d8b9fd26.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "q-160641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa050b2fbc3a232d728e05749f35cdfa9dffb6b86a2929c1eb364bf9a39d4dc4"} {"question" "e-15087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15087.json" "file_sha256" "43069a95196acf0a5054358a6509047fd2fb08e7294bf7daa91c22b0d971138e"} {"question" "e-15087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4.$\n\nFor the side $S_4,$ we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(0-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2},\\,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15087.json" "file_sha256" "43069a95196acf0a5054358a6509047fd2fb08e7294bf7daa91c22b0d971138e"} {"question" "e-15066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15066.json" "file_sha256" "d505791e19cbc0c2aef73d7c73829cfd6eafb40799361d3f7b544cce3cdfc535"} {"question" "e-15066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3.$\n\nFor the side $S_3,$ we have $v=1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2u-3v,\\,{v}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3(1),\\,{1}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3,1-3u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1583f8d3ad69b8d5e3ac051eeb50b55860737a60d462d126ae5dc2b49c3ea155.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15066.json" "file_sha256" "d505791e19cbc0c2aef73d7c73829cfd6eafb40799361d3f7b544cce3cdfc535"} {"question" "e-15064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = ( u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15064.json" "file_sha256" "13a6215f01e8f15f2e5adfcdc048857cd95e2ed208c507b8886517cf5ad9d268"} {"question" "e-15064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1.$\n\nFor the side $S_1,$ we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u+v)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =((u+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny^2=x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to ((0+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{0}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to ((1+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{0+1})=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f94f6fef873efd64d9da9e015e0056944b63846d741e12937507205b118638b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15064.json" "file_sha256" "13a6215f01e8f15f2e5adfcdc048857cd95e2ed208c507b8886517cf5ad9d268"} {"question" "e-15065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15065.json" "file_sha256" "b42916b148fdafb3f4ed7545aa8dac2804b26c638fab4c868ea6ad9926aa71a6"} {"question" "e-15065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$ So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1,$ where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,u(u+0)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2.$ Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$ Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(1(v),\\,1(1+v)) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3.$ Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,u(u+1)) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}+u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4.$ Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,0(0+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/793412e250ac4f26966293771306f28bb037685dd7886dc386706f23dcaee8af.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/example-15065.json" "file_sha256" "b42916b148fdafb3f4ed7545aa8dac2804b26c638fab4c868ea6ad9926aa71a6"}] "usage" {"q-160605" [] "e-15064" [] "e-15066" [] "e-15087" [] "e-15065" [] "q-160353" []} "basis" 1689 "retractions" [[17592186157544 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160605.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = u - v, y = u(1 + v)$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n$x =-t, y = y (t), 1 \\ge t \\ge 0$.\n\nFind the function $y(t)$."] [17592186157540 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = v, y = u(1 + v^{2})$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ$.*"] [17592186156961 :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/301e42965375d20de3dae4fc94252bcb.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n$x = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0$.\n\nFind the function $x(t)$."] [17592186156961 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4$.\n\nFor the side $S_4$, we have $u = 0$ and\n\n$$\n1 \\geq v \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u - v^{2}, 2v) \\\\\n&= (0 - v^{2}, 2v) \\\\\n&= (-v^{2}, 2v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0\n$$"] [17592186156960 :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/eb83daf1d6411acc9b374a6918e705d5.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed."] [17592186156960 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3$.\n\nFor the side $S_3$, we have $v=1$ and\n\n$$\n1 \\geq u \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3v, v^{2} - 3u) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3(1), 1^{2} - 3u) \\\\\n&= (2u - 3, 1 - 3u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/7a1dec23fac3c206ac36d01957cf50c7.png)"] [17592186156958 :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/f5018f5963c09ade7244ff19220fbce9.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n$x = (u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186156958 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1$.\n\nFor the side $S_1$, we have $v = 0$ and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq u \\leq 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= ((u + v)^{2}, \\frac{u}{v + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((u + 0)^{2}, \\frac{u}{0 + 1}) \\\\\n&= (u^{2}, u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n$y^2=x$\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$.\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\nT: (0, 0) → ((0 + 0)^{2}, \\frac{0}{0 + 1}) = (0, 0); T: (1, 0) → ((1 + 0)^{2}, \\frac{1}{0 + 1}) = (1, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/0bfa665ade1f4ccd9b7293a0d1b0370e.png)"] [17592186156959 :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/bb3f4499d045cb59b5d13942268c59c2.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v)$.\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$."] [17592186156959 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta$. So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1$, where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1$. Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (u(0), u(u + 0)); = (0, u^{2}), \\mid 0 \\le u \\le 1$.\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2$. Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1$. Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (1(v), 1(1 + v)); = (v, 1 + v), \\mid 0 \\le v \\le 1$.\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3$. Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0$. Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (u(1), u(u + 1)); = (u, u^{2} + u), \\mid 1 \\ge u \\ge 0$.\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4$. Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0$. Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y)|= T(u, v); = (uv, u(u + v)); = (0v, 0(0 + v)); = (0, 0), \\mid 1 \\ge v \\ge 0$.\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/29a5c447957277442ef4784d7252b080.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186153816 17592186153817 17592186153818 17592186153819 17592186153820 17592186153840 17592186153841 17592186153842 17592186153843 17592186153844] "immutable_fields" [[17592186153815 :answer-field/correct] [17592186153815 :answer-field/id] [17592186153815 :answer-field/key] [17592186153815 :answer-field/type] [17592186153839 :answer-field/correct] [17592186153839 :answer-field/id] [17592186153839 :answer-field/key] [17592186153839 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
